* A authorial / shogunal self-censorship before presentation;
* B moral / social censorship by feudal copyists uncomfortable with kunoichi material under Confucian gender norms;
* D Oniwaban state-secret redaction of operational techniques still in use in the Edo period;
* E aburidashi heat-revealed invisible ink that a non-initiate scribe would see as blank and copy as a placeholder;
* F an oral-aid colour / symbol mnemonic for material transmitted orally, misread as blank by non-initiates.

G is weak on consistency grounds — physical over-handling and ink fade are copy-specific and would produce stochastic, diverging gaps, marginal notes, 欠字/闕, or conjectural restorations, not an identical black/white circle code in every line — but it is mechanistically possible if a single damaged archetype seeded the whole tradition.

