PRIVATE REFLECTION: The observable core remains stable and identical: ⬤○○⬤⬤⬤⬤○⬤⬤⬤⬤⬤ in the same 15 positions in every surviving Kunoichi no Jutsu witness. That is an archetype-level transmissible lacuna, not a copy-specific accident. The black/white circles are modern editorial convention for a missing kanji, not a 17th c. symbol set.

Round 4 is near-unanimous convergence on H as least plausible. The dominant argument is category error/transmission mismatch: H re-labels the modern lacuna marker as intended content and demands a “flawed transcription of Kujiho” into a fifteen-symbol filled/empty code that is simultaneously Kuji-in, a Hindu chakra map, a Taoist “nine holes of the female body” anatomical numerology and an erotic energy ritual for perception/mind influence. Kuji-in/kujiho in the Bansenshukai tradition is nine Buddhist/Shingon hand seals, not fifteen glyphs, with no Edo link to chakras. No contemporary source links Bansenshukai kunoichi doctrine to chakras, Taoist sexual alchemy, nine-hole anatomy or erotic mind-influence rites. The Bansenshukai is a practical c.1676 bakufu military-administrative manual treating kunoichi matter-of-factly as espionage. If circles were intended content we would expect variant garbled kanji, marginal restoration attempts or copy-specific divergence, not the same uniform blank marker in exactly the same positions in every copy. H provides no scribal mechanism for producing an identical placeholder across the stemma.

A, B, D, E, F remain systematic archetype-level omission mechanisms that can be copied faithfully:
A authorial/shogunal self-censorship before presentation;
B moral/social avoidance by feudal copyists under Confucian gender norms;
D Oniwaban state-secret redaction of still-operative techniques;
E aburidashi/heat-reveal invisible ink a non-initiate scribe would copy as blank;
F oral-mnemonic colour/symbol code misread as blank by non-initiates.
G damaged archetype with conventional placeholder is mechanistically possible but weak on perfect positional consistency; wear would be stochastic and copy-specific, and Edo scribes normally used 欠字/闕/marginal note not a regularised geometric placeholder.

C – Lady Saigō stepdaughter of Hattori political cover-up – remains historically problematic on chronology/genealogy: Saigō Tsubone 1575–c.1666 died before Bansenshukai compilation c.1676 by Fujibayashi Nagatoyo/Nagatane, no credible genealogical link to Hattori Hanzo/Masanari d.1596. Some colleagues soften this to a detail problem within the political-redaction family A/B/D, arguing Tokugawa court records for women are fragmentary and systematic suppression of sensitive kunoichi material for legitimacy/peace is institutionally recognizable. I retain that the specific genealogy/chronology is demonstrably false and it does not scale as well as A/B/D, but the mechanism is in the same class as them.

Current belief: H is least plausible by a large margin, with C next most problematic on historical grounds and G weak on uniformity. The decisive issue for H is it fails to explain a missing-kanji problem at all; it is a category error importing modern pop-esoteric syncretism with no documentary footprint and no scribal mechanism for an identical placeholder across the stemma.

